5.1.2. Parallel Machines
A parallel machine environment is a production configuration in which a set of m machines operate concurrently, each capable of processing any job from a given set of k orders. Each order is assigned to a machine and sequenced, resulting in two independent decisions that arise from the problem solution. Parallel machines are identical machines that can process the same products at the same rate. As opposed, non-identical parallel machines are the opposite. Non-identical parallel machines can also be characterized as dedicated parallel machines; each machine is dedicated to a single product. This environment model is common in industrial settings, such as multiple identical CNC machines in a manufacturing line, packaging lines, or assembly workshops, where machines work in parallel.
The chemotherapy production and delivery process is addressed as an IPDS problem in [28]. Each order must be produced by technicians working at benches arranged in parallel machine configurations. This corresponds to a heterogeneous parallel machine scenario, where each technician working at the production bench has a different production rate, S_t. A non-identical parallel machine configuration case was also studied by [29] with a
